# Windows PowerShell scripts: CRLF line endings. The files are committed with a
# UTF-8 BOM as part of their content — Windows PowerShell 5.1 otherwise decodes
# BOM-less files as Windows-1252 and corrupts non-ASCII characters inside string
# literals (which breaks parsing). Do not strip the BOM when editing.
#
# EXCEPTION — every PowerShell file reachable by `irm <url> | iex` must stay
# BOM-LESS and ASCII-only. irm keeps a BOM as a stray U+FEFF that makes iex fail
# on the first token (fixed in 762dcc0, regressed once by re-applying this rule
# mechanically). ASCII-only keeps them safe to run from a local clone too.
#
# The exempt set is:
#   windows/install.ps1     windows/uninstall.ps1     (published one-liner URLs)
#   install/install.ps1     install/uninstall.ps1     (the bodies they delegate to)
#
# `fetched_powershell_installers_are_bomless_ascii` in tests/equivalence.rs
# asserts this, so a mechanical re-application fails the suite rather than
# shipping.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Unix shell scripts must keep LF, or shebangs break on Linux/macOS.
*.sh  text eol=lf

*.md   text
*.json text

*.png binary

# --- Fixture harness (U3) -------------------------------------------------
# U14 owns rewriting the encoding regime for the single-crate layout. These
# rules are scoped to the paths the harness reads and writes, because getting
# them wrong corrupts data rather than merely annoying an editor.
#
# Harness inputs are pinned to LF on every platform. R30 requires a case to pin
# every input it supplies, and an input whose bytes depend on the checkout is
# not pinned: a transcript arriving CRLF on Windows and LF on Linux feeds two
# different token counts into what is supposed to be one fixture.
tests/harness/cases.json     text eol=lf
tests/harness/states.json    text eol=lf
tests/harness/payloads/**    text eol=lf
tests/harness/inputs/**      text eol=lf
tests/harness/configs/**     text eol=lf

# cmd.exe needs CRLF in a batch file.
tests/harness/shims/record.cmd text eol=crlf

# Golden captures are bytes, not text. Any normalisation here would rewrite the
# very output the fixture exists to preserve, and the diff would look like the
# scripts changed.
tests/fixtures/**/expected/** -text
tests/fixtures/**/case.json  text eol=lf
